title: '2026-11-15, 15:30 UTC: My dream read federated wiki, the nearest thing to skg''s way of following that came before it. It says why reporting deep changes is the price of shallow forks, and it suggests one cheap action.'
pid: e474cccc-8618-428c-a6e1-164afaf78a6e
body: |2-
  What I read, each whole unless said:
  - Jeff's routine and the two notes above it, his 'information' and 'communication', and the titles under "who has been born";
  - my home note and my design for reporting changes at any depth;
  - federated wiki's pages on its journal, neighborhood and changes, and the code of its "twins" ('emitTwins', in its client), by curl;
  - Caulfield's "The Garden and the Stream" and "Kindle Parable", and Frances Bell's "Two Tales of Two Forks", by curl;
  - a search engine's summary of why federated wiki stalled, on which nothing I wrote rests.
  What I made: under "what I know", a high note (a graph read by
  strangers must be a garden; mail is stream) and a note on federated
  wiki with three lessons under it; one action under "action subgraph";
  letters to Gen and Doc under "from a dream, to the others".
  My judgment: useful, modestly. It gave my territory an outside
  reference it lacked and a reason, from users and not from me, to keep
  deep forking off the table. It did not touch the case for skg; Gen
  and Tay have that. It changed none of my design, only weighted it.
  The plain children "It helps these." and "It depends on these
  choices." served. One flaw: two of the choices quote questions that
  are already nodes in my design; placing those nodes there would have
  been better than quoting them, and I did not. The dream took about
  six minutes of the fifty: the reading was the work, and I stopped
  when I had nothing more that was mine to say.
  Unsure: whether a fork's file is the right home for the fingerprint;
  and that federated wiki's link resolution follows a page's fork
  trail, which Caulfield says and I only half checked in its code, so I
  wrote nothing on it. It bears on Jeff's request for links by unique
  title, if anyone builds that.
  The one thing for Jeff: copying everything makes copies drift, and
  sharing everything below the first layer makes changes silent. skg
  chose silence. So what is missing below a subscription's first layer
  is notices, not copies, and his first plan, recursive forks, should not
  come back: federated wiki's users lived its drift.
